Answer Questions Q6.00.1, Q6.00.2 and Q6.00.3 using the following data. These three questions together carry a total of <strong>5 marks</strong>. Open-loop transfer function of a unity-feedback system is: \[G(s)=G_1(s)e^{-s\tau_D}
=
\frac{e^{-s\tau_D}}
{s(s+1)(s+2)}\] Given: \(|G_1(j\omega)|=1\) when \(\omega=0.466.\)<br/><br/>Determine the phase margin when \(\tau_D=0\).

Answer Questions Q6.00.1, Q6.00.2 and Q6.00.3 using the following data. These three questions together carry a total of <strong>5 marks</strong>. Open-loop transfer function of a unity-feedback system is: \[G(s)=G_1(s)e^{-s\tau_D}
=
\frac{e^{-s\tau_D}}
{s(s+1)(s+2)}\] Given: \(|G_1(j\omega)|=1\) when \(\omega=0.466.\)<br/><br/>Comment in one sentence on the effect of dead time on the stability of the system.

questions.explanation:
The delay term in the open-loop transfer function is \(e^{-s\tau_D}\) \(=\) \(e^{-j\omega\tau_D}\) Its magnitude is \(\left|e^{-j\omega\tau_D}\right|=1,\) so the gain of the system remains unchanged. However, it introduces an additional phase lag of \(\angle e^{-j\omega\tau_D}=-\omega\tau_D \ \text{rad}.\) Hence, the phase margin in the presence of dead time becomes \[\text{PM}_{\text{new}}
=
\text{PM}_{\text{old}}
-
\omega_{gc}\tau_D.\] Therefore, as the dead time \(\tau_D\) increases, the additional phase lag increases, causing the phase margin to decrease. Consequently, the stability of the closed-loop system decreases. Hence, \[\boxed{\text{As dead time increases, the stability decreases}}\]

questions.question:
Answer Questions Q6.00.1, Q6.00.2 and Q6.00.3 using the following data. These three questions together carry a total of <strong>5 marks</strong>. Open-loop transfer function of a unity-feedback system is: \[G(s)=G_1(s)e^{-s\tau_D}
=
\frac{e^{-s\tau_D}}
{s(s+1)(s+2)}\] Given: \(|G_1(j\omega)|=1\) when \(\omega=0.466.\)<br/><br/>Determine the maximum value of dead time \(\tau_D\) for the closed-loop system to be stable.

questions.explanation:
From Q6.00.1, the phase margin without dead time is \[\text{PM}_{\text{old}}
=
51.91^\circ
=
\frac{51.91\pi}{180} \text{ rad}
=
0.906~\text{rad}\] From Q6.00.2, \[\text{PM}_{\text{new}}
=
\text{PM}_{\text{old}}
-
\omega_{gc}\tau_D\] The maximum allowable dead time corresponds to the stability limit, where the phase margin becomes zero. \[0
=
\text{PM}_{\text{old}}
-
\omega_{gc}\tau_{D,\max}\] \[\omega_{gc}\tau_{D,\max}
=
\text{PM}_{\text{old}}\] \[\tau_{D,\max}
=
\frac{\text{PM}_{\text{old}}}{\omega_{gc}}\] Substituting \(\omega_{gc}=0.466~\text{rad/s},\) \[\tau_{D,\max}
=
\frac{0.906}{0.466}
=
1.944~\text{s}\] Hence, \[\boxed{\tau_{D,\max}\approx1.94~\text{s}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div>The characteristic equation of the closed-loop system is \(s(s+1)(s+2)+e^{-s\tau_D}=0\). Using the first-order approximation, \(e^{-s\tau_D}\approx 1-s\tau_D\), the characteristic equation becomes \[s^3+3s^2+(2-\tau_D)s+1=0\] The Routh array is \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
s^3 &amp; 1 &amp; 2-\tau_D\\
s^2 &amp; 3 &amp; 1\\ s^1 &amp; \dfrac{5-3\tau_D}{3} &amp; 0\\ s^0 &amp; 1 &amp;
\end{array}\] For stability, \[\frac{5-3\tau_D}{3}&gt;0\] \[\tau_D&lt;\frac{5}{3}\] Hence, the maximum permissible dead time is \[\tau_{D,\max}=\frac{5}{3}\,\text{s}=1.67\,\text{s}\] The above result is obtained using the first-order approximation \(e^{-s\tau_D}\approx 1-s\tau_D,\) which is valid only when \(|s\tau_D|\ll1\). In this problem, \(\omega_{gc}\tau_{D,\max}\approx0.9~\text{rad},\) which is not sufficiently small. Hence, the approximation introduces a noticeable error, giving \(\tau_{D,\max}\approx1.67~\text{s},\) whereas the more accurate value is \(\tau_{D,\max}\approx1.94~\text{s},\) obtained directly from the phase margin relation. Since the gain crossover frequency is already given, the phase margin method is the preferred approach here.</div>

questions.question:
Answer Questions Q9.00.1, Q9.00.2 and Q9.00.3 using the following data. These three questions together carry a total of <strong>5 marks</strong>. Consider the state equation \[\dot{x}(t)=Ax(t)\] Given: \[e^{At}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
e^{-t}+te^{-t} &amp; te^{-t}\\
-te^{-t} &amp; e^{-t}-te^{-t}
\end{bmatrix}\]<br/><br/>Find a set of states \(x_1(1)\) and \(x_2(1)\) such that \(x_1(2)=2\).

questions.explanation:
The state response is \[x(t)=e^{At}x(0)\] Given, \[e^{At}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
e^{-t}+te^{-t} &amp; te^{-t}\\
-te^{-t} &amp; e^{-t}-te^{-t}
\end{bmatrix}\] Therefore, \[\begin{bmatrix}
x_1(t)\\
x_2(t)
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
e^{-t}+te^{-t} &amp; te^{-t}\\
-te^{-t} &amp; e^{-t}-te^{-t}
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1(0)\\
x_2(0)
\end{bmatrix}\] At \(t=1\), \[\begin{bmatrix}
x_1(1)\\
x_2(1)
\end{bmatrix}
=
e^{-1}
\begin{bmatrix}
2 &amp; 1\\
-1 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1(0)\\
x_2(0)
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
e^{-1}\left(2x_1(0)+x_2(0)\right)\\
-e^{-1}x_1(0)
\end{bmatrix}\] \[x_1(1)
=
e^{-1}
\left(
2x_1(0)+x_2(0)
\right)\] \[x_2(1)
=
-e^{-1}x_1(0)\] Since \[x(2)=e^{2A}x(0)=e^A(e^{A}x(0))=e^Ax(1),\] \[x_1(2)
=
e^{-1}
\left(
2x_1(1)+x_2(1)
\right)\] Substituting the values of \(x_1(1)\) and \(x_2(1)\), \[x_1(2)
=
e^{-1}
\left[
2e^{-1}
\left(
2x_1(0)+x_2(0)
\right)
-
e^{-1}x_1(0)
\right]
=
e^{-2}
\left[
4x_1(0)+2x_2(0)-x_1(0)
\right]
=
e^{-2}
\left[
3x_1(0)+2x_2(0)
\right]\] Given, \[x_1(2)=2\] \[e^{-2}
\left[
3x_1(0)+2x_2(0)
\right]
=
2\] \[\therefore\qquad
3x_1(0)+2x_2(0)=2e^2\] Hence, all initial states satisfying \(3x_1(0)+2x_2(0)=2e^2\), produce the set of states: \[x_1(1)
=
e^{-1}
\left(
2x_1(0)+x_2(0)
\right)\] \[x_2(1)
=
-e^{-1}x_1(0)\]

