As the motor is a permanent magnet DC motor, the field flux remains constant for any load current. \[T=kI_{a}\] \[E=k\omega\] At no-load, friction and windage losses are neglected. Therefore, \[I_{a}=0\] and \[E_{1}=V_{dc}=120\,\text{V}\] Given no-load speed, \[N_{1}=6000\,\text{rpm}\] \[\omega_{1}=\frac{2\pi}{60}\times 6000\] Hence, \[120=k\left(\frac{2\pi}{60}\times 6000\right)\] \[k=0.191\,\frac{\text{V}\cdot\text{s}}{\text{rad}}\] For developed torque, \[T=0.5\,\text{N}\cdot\text{m}\] \[I_{a}=\frac{T}{k}
      =\frac{0.5}{0.191}
      =2.617\,\text{A}\] When terminal voltage is reduced to \(60\,\text{V}\), \[E_{2}=60-(2.617)(2.5)\] \[E_{2}=53.45\,\text{V}\] Therefore, \[\omega_{2}=\frac{E_{2}}{k}
=\frac{53.45}{0.191}
=279.87\,\text{rad/s}\] Converting to rpm, \[N_{2}=\frac{60}{2\pi}\omega_{2}\] \[N_{2}
