For the production of steady torque in an induction machine, the rotor must have the same number of poles as the stator. Hence, \[\text{Number of rotor poles} = \text{Number of stator poles} = 4\] The number of rotor phases is given by \[\text{Number of rotor phases}
=
\frac{\text{Number of rotor slots}}
{\text{Number of rotor poles}}\] \[=
\frac{28}{4}
=
7\] Therefore, \[\boxed{C}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Stator and rotor must have the same number of poles to develop steady electromagnetic torque.</li><li>Rotor phase number can be determined from the ratio of rotor slots to rotor poles.</li><li>In a polyphase wound rotor, adjacent slots belonging to one phase form a repeating pattern over the pole pitch.</li></ul></div>

