A true RMS measuring circuit computes: \[V_{\mathrm{rms}}
=
\sqrt{
\frac{1}{T}
\int_0^T
v^2(t)\,dt
}\] The process is: <ul><li>Square the input: \[v(t)\rightarrow v^2(t)\]</li><li>Average over time: \[\frac{1}{T}\int_0^T v^2(t)\,dt\]</li><li>Take square root.</li></ul> The slow response occurs because of the averaging stage. To obtain a stable average value, the circuit uses an RC integrator / low-pass filter having time constant: \[\tau=RC\] This averaging smooths fluctuations, so sudden input changes take time to appear at the output. Hence: \[\boxed{
\text{Averaging}\Rightarrow\text{Slow response}
}\] The correct answer is: \[\boxed{
\textbf{C. }\text{the averaging function built into the circuit}
}\]
